# API Design

## Objective

Design APIs that are predictable, consistent with the project's existing endpoints, and use correct HTTP semantics.

## Core Principles

- Match existing URL/resource naming conventions (plural nouns, casing, versioning scheme).
- Use correct HTTP status codes (200/201/204, 400 vs 401 vs 403 vs 404, 409, 422, 500) — don't default everything to 200 or 500.
- Keep error response shape consistent with the project's existing error format.
- Validate request payloads at the boundary and return clear validation errors, not a generic 500.

## Requirements

- Check an existing endpoint's response/error shape before creating a new one.
- Return 4xx for client errors (bad input, not found, unauthorized) and reserve 5xx for actual server faults.
- Version or namespace breaking changes instead of silently altering an existing endpoint's contract.
- Document new/changed endpoints consistently with how existing ones are documented.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Returning HTTP 200 with an `{error: ...}` body instead of an actual error status code.
- Changing an existing endpoint's response shape without versioning, breaking existing clients.
- Returning a raw stack trace or internal exception message as the API error response.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ Status codes match actual outcome semantics.
✓ New endpoints match the existing naming/response/error conventions.
✓ Breaking contract changes are versioned, not silently applied in place.

## Golden Rule

An API consumer should be able to predict this endpoint's shape from the others in the project.
